derekt75 <derek.truesdale@anritsu.com> writes:

>> The PR is a dead end; has always been.
>
> I thought it worked well for my healers.

I consider it to be a false economy, because surviving long enough to
actually make it to Minetown is the hardest, most dangerous part of
the
game, and the protection racket makes it harder and more dangerous.

Someone else mentions that it's useful for certain multi-conduct
games,
and I can see that.  Particularly, if you're playing a pacifist
conduct
anyhow, the additional sacrifice (in survivability) that you make to
also do the protection racket is relatively marginal.

But for a non-conduct game, the protection racket is a substantial
hurdle to early-game survival.  Actually, the protection racket is in
some ways very similar to conduct play.

Bear in mind that if you do survive past Minetown, gold becomes more
and
more plentiful all the time, the further into the game you get, so you
WILL eventually buy all the protection the priests will sell you
(unless
your pet kills all the priests; learning to prevent that is a separate
issue), plus you will also buy every item from every shop in the whole
dungeon, and you will still have more gold left over than it's worth
your while to carry on the ascension run.  That's if you *don't* have
your pets kill any "extra" shopkeepers or priests (by extra, I mean
ones
you don't need alive for price identification or protection buying;
you
may also consider Izchak and co-aligned priests not to be extra, for
sentimental reasons).

Yes, succeeding at the protection racket gives you more protection on
your first trip to Minetown, so it gives you a bigger increase in
survivability at that time.  Naturally, you will feel like it's
working,
because when you do manage to actually get to Minetown the game
suddenly
gets a lot easier to survive than it was a few turns earlier.

In return, however, it demands that you get all the way to Minetown as
an XL1 character, which is much harder than getting there in one piece
after gaining a few levels, and then once you do get there you're
still
rather behind in terms of gaining levels.  The levels you've not
gained
would also have survivability benefits (ongoing ones, as well as on
the
way there), not least a rather substantial chunk of max HP.

I do think it's advantageous, once you have cleared the way to a level
with an aligned priest (and have enough food or a ring of SD), to make
trips there every time you accumulate enough gold to buy another point
of protection.  Sometimes I choose to wait until I have enough to buy
multiple points, but that's for convenience, to make the game less
tedious; it's not theoretically optimal strategy.

Selling things you don't need right now but will want later (e.g.,
spellbooks you can't usefully read yet) to buy protection is also
advantageous, even though it costs you gold in the long run, because
gold in the long run is not in short supply.  Having the protection
now
is worth paying twice the zorkmid count you sold the stuff for when
you
rebuy it later, because inflation as you descend into the mine and
gold
becomes more common means that in practice 1000 zorkmids when you've
reached Gehennom is actually worth a lot less, in real terms, than 500
zorkmids when you've just reached Minetown.  Again, I don't always
actually do this, but it is sound strategy nonetheless -- even
ignoring
the possibility of later taming a dragon or whatnot and doing the
magic
whistle assasination trick on the shopkeeper.


